        "abstract": "<jats:p>\n                    Fix an element\n                    <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\n                      <mml:mi>\u03b1</mml:mi>\n                    </mml:math>\n                    in a quadratic field\n                    <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\n                      <mml:mi>K</mml:mi>\n                    </mml:math>\n                    . Define\n                    <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\n                      <mml:mi>S</mml:mi>\n                    </mml:math>\n                    as the set of rational primes\n                    <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\n                      <mml:mi>p</mml:mi>\n                    </mml:math>\n                    , for which\n                    <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\n                      <mml:mi>\u03b1</mml:mi>\n                    </mml:math>\n                    has maximal order modulo\n                    <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\n                      <mml:mi>p</mml:mi>\n                    </mml:math>\n                    . Under the assumption of the generalized Riemann hypothesis, we show that\n                    <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\n                      <mml:mi>S</mml:mi>\n                    </mml:math>\n                    has a density. Moreover, we give necessary and sufficient conditions for the density of\n                    <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\n                      <mml:mi>S</mml:mi>\n                    </mml:math>\n                    to be positive.\n                  </jats:p>",
